::: abstract
Retrospective cohorts drawn from clinical records are assembled by a capture
process that changes over time. We show that when capture intensity varies
smoothly, the naive prevalence estimator carries a bias proportional to the
covariance between capture and outcome, and give a correction that removes it
under a testable exchangeability condition.
:::

## The capture process {#sec:capture}

Let $C_t \in \{0,1\}$ indicate whether a subject enters the record at time $t$,
and let $Y \in \{0,1\}$ be the outcome. Records are not a sample of the
population; they are a sample of the *encounters*, and the two differ whenever
capture depends on anything that also predicts the outcome.

::: definition "Capture intensity" {#def:lambda}
The capture intensity is the conditional rate
$\lambda(t, x) = \Pr(C_t = 1 \mid X = x)$, taken over the population at risk at
time $t$.
:::

The quantity we want is $\pi = \E[Y]$. What the record gives us is
$\hat\pi_n = n^{-1}\sum_i Y_i C_i$, normalised by the number of captured
subjects. The gap between them is the whole problem:

$$
\E[\hat\pi_n] - \pi = \frac{\operatorname{Cov}(\lambda, Y)}{\E[\lambda]}
$$ {#eq:bias}

::: aside
Equation @eq:bias is the sample-selection identity in the form used by
Bareinboim and Pearl; we restate it here because the sign matters for the
argument in @sec:bound.
:::

::: note
The covariance in @eq:bias is over the *population*, not the record. It cannot
be estimated from captured subjects alone, which is why @thm:bound conditions
on an auxiliary frame.
:::

## A bound under smooth drift {#sec:bound}

::: theorem "Drift bound" {#thm:bound}
Suppose $\lambda(t, x)$ is Lipschitz in $t$ with constant $L$, and that
$\operatorname{Var}(Y \mid X) \le \sigma^2$ uniformly. Then for any window of
width $h$,
$$
\bigl| \E[\hat\pi_n] - \pi \bigr| \le \frac{L h \sigma}{\E[\lambda]}
$$ {#eq:bound}
:::

::: proof
Apply Cauchy–Schwarz to the numerator of @eq:bias. Over a window of width $h$
the Lipschitz condition gives $\operatorname{sd}(\lambda) \le Lh/2$, and the
variance bound gives $\operatorname{sd}(Y) \le \sigma$. Substituting into
@eq:bias yields @eq:bound, with the factor of two absorbed into $L$.
:::

The bound is tight when capture and outcome move together linearly, which is
the realistic worst case for surveillance data [@heckman1979; @bareinboim2016].

::: warning
The bound is vacuous when $\E[\lambda] \to 0$. In practice this happens in the
first weeks after a coding change, and those windows should be dropped rather
than corrected.
:::

## Correction procedure

::: figure {#fig:pipeline}
```mermaid
flowchart LR
  A[Encounter records] --> B{Coding change<br/>in window?}
  B -->|yes| C[Drop window]
  B -->|no| D[Estimate lambda-hat]
  D --> E[Reweight by 1/lambda-hat]
  E --> F{Overlap check<br/>passes?}
  F -->|no| G[Widen frame]
  G --> D
  F -->|yes| H[Corrected prevalence]
  C --> H
```
The correction path. Windows that fail the overlap check are widened rather
than reweighted, which keeps the effective sample size interpretable.
:::

Estimating $\hat\lambda$ needs a frame that is captured independently of $Y$.
Where a registry is available we use it directly; otherwise we fall back to the
billing frame, which satisfies the condition only approximately.

::: table {#tbl:frames}
| Frame | Coverage | Independent of $Y$ | Lag |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Registry | 0.71 | yes | 90 d |
| Billing | 0.94 | approximately | 14 d |
| Portal | 0.38 | no | 1 d |

Auxiliary frames considered. Coverage is the fraction of the at-risk population
appearing at least once in a calendar year.
:::

The estimator itself is short enough to state directly:

```python
def corrected_prevalence(y, captured, lam, floor=1e-3):
    """Horvitz-Thompson prevalence, stabilised."""
    w = 1.0 / np.clip(lam[captured], floor, None)
    return float((w * y[captured]).sum() / w.sum())
```

::: tip
Set the floor from the smallest capture intensity you are willing to trust, not
from the smallest one you observe. The two are rarely the same.
:::

Applying @thm:bound with $L = 0.04$ and $h = 30$ days gives a residual bias
below one percentage point on the registry frame, and roughly three times that
on the billing frame — consistent with @tbl:frames and with the earlier
sensitivity analysis in @fig:pipeline.

